### Kernel gradients

$\frac{\partial L}{\partial k^{(o,c)}_{mn}} = \sum_{i,j} \delta^{(o)}_{ij} \cdot \tilde{x}^{(c)}_{i s_h + m,\; j s_w + n}$: the kernel gradient is a cross-correlation of the padded input with $\delta$, sampled at stride positions. Fully expanded for the top left element of filter 0, channel R, grouped by output rows (zero products are omitted):

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial k^{(0,R)}_{00}} = {{.DWAlignedBody}}
\end{aligned} \tag{ {{- .DWAlignedTag -}} }$$

Every product pairs a $\delta$ element with the padded input value under the kernel position $(0,0)$ of the corresponding window. The remaining elements follow the same pattern:

{{range .GradWBlocks}}$$\frac{\partial L}{\partial k^{({{.Name}})}} = {{.Tex}} \tag{ {{- .Tag -}} }$$

{{end}}### Input gradients

Every output element $\delta^{(o)}_{ij}$ scatters the kernel slice $k^{(o,c)}$ (multiplied by $\delta^{(o)}_{ij}$) back onto the padded canvas at the window position, contributions accumulate, the padding ring is cropped afterwards. Since the kernel is larger than the stride, neighbouring windows overlap: with kernel width {{.KernelW}} and stride {{.StrideW}} an interior pixel is covered by up to 3 windows horizontally and 2 vertically, from both filters. For the pixel at row {{.PixRow}}, column {{.PixCol}} of channel R (position row {{.PixPadRow}}, column {{.PixPadCol}} of the padded canvas):

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x^{(R)}_{ {{- .PixRow}}{{.PixCol -}} }} = {{.PixBody}}
\end{aligned} \tag{ {{- .PixTag -}} }$$

(the first line collects the windows of filter 0, the second one of filter 1; zero products are omitted; the subscripts under the braces name the contributing window and the kernel element). Full input gradients after accumulating every window of both filters and cropping the padding:

